 "verdict": {
  "ide_tests": "20 collected / 20 passed with --tb=no (PROGRESS-ide-qoder.md:456 claims 19; the extra one is a real collected test, count is off by 1)",
  "ide_tests_wallclock_s": 71.34,
  "sidecar": "PRODUCED at nlcsplit/logs/fastpair_ide_validation.json (--tier l1 --out <that path>): 2 rows, (H2O)2 lvl1 and (C6H6)2 lvl0, both with n_dropped_screen=0, bound_ha=0.0, l1_pass=true, l1_probe=null. The min_block_bound_ha values in it (197.95986835849376 Ha) were reproduced independently in this run.",
  "ide_l2_tier": "check_screen_bound_ide.py --tier all / --tier l2 CRASHES in this tree: its default S22 dir is <repo>/s22 which does not exist here (real dir is <repo>/scratch/lit/s22). Setting S22DIR makes L2 run (水二聚体 PASS 119s, 氨二聚体 PASS 178s before this session's time-box cut it off). Their cross-system acceptance rows are therefore not reproducible from the repo as shipped.",
  "full_path_equivalence": "ours and ide agree with the full O(N^2) anchor to 1e-16..1e-17 Ha on every system: the shared kernel, same-centre pairing and x2 bookkeeping are correct in both.",
  "ide_l1_status": "FAILS the criterion as a test: it PASSES L1 only vacuously. n_dropped_screen=0 at eps=1e-8 Ha on all three systems, certified bound sum = 0.0 Ha, actual dropped = 0.0 Ha (0 >= 0). Even eps=100 Ha screens nothing; the smallest block bound is 134-261 Ha, i.e. 1e10x above the tolerance. Only at eps=1000 Ha does it drop 7-12 blocks out of 21-300.",
  "ours_l1_status": "PASSES L1 non-vacuously at tau=1e-8 Ha on all three systems: cert_err > 0 and >= the actual dropped amount, while dropping 52-60% of the far point pairs.",
  "who_is_tighter": "At the atom-block granularity ide actually delivers (one block per atom, 21/21/300 blocks) the two bound functions are within 1.2x of each other and ide's is the smaller on both water rows (197.96 vs 221.32 Ha; 133.93 vs 162.09 Ha) and identical on benzene lvl0 (188.32 Ha). Neither is remotely usable there. Operationally ours is tighter by construction because it refines below the atom block into a tree, which is the only reason ours screens anything at tau=1e-8 Ha.",
  "external_anchor_A_full_ON2": "both pass; identical to anchor within 1e-17 Ha.",
  "external_anchor_B_pyscf_native": "both pass. Water lvl1: E_total vs native = -1.5e-3 / -8e-4 kcal/mol (grid difference). Benzene lvl0 the two grids differ by +0.41 kcal/mol, i.e. level 0 is not converged for the benzene NLC term -- this limits the anchor, not either implementation.",
  "external_anchor_C_orca": {
   "water": "tested on the anchor's own geometry (S22_02, level 1). Both implementations give dnlc = -0.593673 kcal/mol vs ORCA -0.593094, deviation 5.8e-4 kcal/mol, consistent with the 4.0e-4 offset orca_anchor.json already documents. PASS for both -- but the anchor cannot discriminate them, because at eps=1e-8 both reduce to the unscreened path.",
   "benzene": "NOT testable at the anchor's level here (level 1 full O(N^2) is the hours-long pole; their own --l2-skip exists for it). At level 0 both give -4.438621 kcal/mol against the same-level external record -4.448 (nlcsplit/logs/20260928T193520Z_centos-step_h_benz_l0.out), deviation 9.4e-3, and -0.113 vs the level-1 ORCA value -4.3252, which is level sensitivity not implementation error."
  },
  "sidecar_d_orca_kcal_reconciliation": "Their sidecar's only real-system row is the GEOMLIB-BUILT water dimer (full_pfrz -0.5236534 kcal/mol, which this run reproduces exactly as the inter-fragment P_frz). The ORCA anchor -0.5931 is defined on the S22_02 official geometry, and P_frz is not the same quantity as dnlc either. On that geometry/quantity pair this run measures geomlib dnlc = -0.515327 (double sum) / -0.516360 (native) vs S22 dnlc = -0.593673, and S22 P_frz = -0.601215, so a 0.068-0.078 kcal/mol spread is fully explained by geometry+quantity mismatch. The exact 0.07828029545 could NOT be reconstructed from anything the return values expose -> UNVERIFIED. What is verified: the deviation is not an implementation error, and no same-level same-quantity ORCA comparison appears in their sidecar.",
  "unverified": [
   "ide's exact definition of d_orca_kcal and d_pyscf_native_kcal inside check_screen_bound_ide.py (source not read by protocol); only the 0.07828 value is observable and it does not match any same-quantity combination I can form.",
   "ide's tier l2 full row set (crashed on the default S22 path in this tree).",
   "whether ide's bound function uses box or true-minimum separation at the atom block (its numbers are 1.2x below ours on water, so they are not bit-identical formulas; not inspected)."
  ],
  "can_A2b_be_accepted": "NO as delivered, and the honest reason is not a wrong number -- it is that the deliverable does nothing. The kernel/decomposition half is correct (reproduces all three external anchors to <=1e-3 kcal/mol). The screening half, which is what A2b was for, screens 0 of 21/21/300 blocks at the claimed eps=1e-8 Ha, screens 0 even at 100 Ha, and its L1 'proof' is the vacuous inequality 0 >= 0. Their own PROGRESS-ide-qoder.md:521-526 already says exactly this and retracts the earlier 'eps=1e-3 screens blocks' claim, and asks for finer blocks (B2). So: correct-but-inert, must not be cited as a delivered bound+screening result; the sidecar's l1_pass=true must not be read as the mechanism working.",
  "tighter": "at equal granularity (one block per atom) ide is marginally tighter on both water rows (197.96 vs 221.32 Ha; 133.93 vs 162.09 Ha) and identical on benzene lvl0 (188.32 Ha both). Neither can screen: the smallest bound is 10-13 orders of magnitude above tau=1e-8 Ha. Ours is the only version that fires at tau=1e-8 Ha, and it does so by refining below the atom block, not by a tighter bound."
